<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Bit-Hamban</h1><p class="st-meta">850 BCE – 700 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Zagros principality on the great road from Mesopotamia into Iran, repeatedly raided and finally annexed by Assyria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bit-Hamban lay in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>, probably around the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river">Diyala</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kermanshah">Kermanshah</a> region, along the route later called the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> Road. It appears in Assyrian annals from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> in the mid-9th century BCE as a land of local lords.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nothing is known of its institutions beyond Assyrian campaign reports, which list towns captured and tribute of horses and livestock. Its rulers seem to have been local chiefs rather than kings of a centralised state, sometimes in league with neighbours such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/parsua">Parsua</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ellipi">Ellipi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tiglath-Pileser III incorporated Bit-Hamban into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> in 744 BCE, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> it formed part of the Assyrian provincial system in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a>. It disappears from the record by about 700 BCE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 850 BCE – 700 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:42.79%;width:2.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>850 BCE – 700 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bit Hamban, Hamban</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-775&amp;focus=bit-hamban" aria-label="Bit-Hamban on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6093 1000 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bit-Hamban lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6413 1256h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6413 1256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iran. <a href="/atlas?year=-775&amp;focus=bit-hamban">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

